WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
API design can reduce use-after-free risk by making lifetime rules visible.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:14.200
A function boundary is where callers learn whether they are creating, borrowing, retaining, releasing, or destroying a resource.

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:20.220
If the boundary is vague, every caller has to guess, and those guesses will eventually diverge.

4
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.220
Naming conventions can clarify intent.

5
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.240
A create or allocate function commonly returns ownership.

6
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.980
An init function may initialize caller-provided storage.

7
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:38.480
A borrow or get function may return a pointer that remains valid only while the owning object remains alive.

8
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.380
A destroy or cleanup function should define what happens to the object and its fields afterward.

9
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:48.060
Functions that take ownership should say so explicitly.

10
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:54.800
If a function consumes a pointer, the caller should not use that pointer afterward except according to the documented contract.

11
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.500
If a function only borrows, it should not release the pointer.

12
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:04.600
If a function retains a pointer, it should document whether it copies the data or stores the pointer itself.

13
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.200
Functions that return borrowed pointers should document how long the pointer remains valid.

14
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:17.700
A returned pointer might be valid until the next mutation, until the owner is destroyed, until the next call, or only during the current callback.

15
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.880
Without that rule, callers may store references that outlive the object.

16
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.680
Structured resource types can keep state, ownership, and cleanup behavior together.

17
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:34.660
A struct can hold the pointer, count, capacity, initialized flag, and ownership mode.

18
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:42.200
Helper functions can then operate on that structure consistently instead of spreading lifecycle rules across unrelated call sites.

19
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.720
Consistent create and destroy, or init and cleanup, pairs make resource lifecycle easier to review.

20
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:57.460
The pair should document who calls each side, whether repeated cleanup is allowed, what state remains after cleanup, and whether borrowed references become invalid.

21
00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:03.800
Consistency is not bureaucracy; it is how reviewers build confidence in lifetime behavior.
